  :adr/body "## The fork this closes

`~/.local/bin/itonami` resolves through `~/.cloud-itonami/current`, and on
2026-09-06 that pointed at `releases/dfddb86b…-clean` -- a build of
`agent/harness-plugin-architecture`, a branch 11 commits ahead of `main` and
41 behind it. Three pointers, three answers: the CLI ran the branch, the
resident server ran `releases/e97b6ed…` (the merge-base, up 2 days 11 hours),
and `main` had moved past both.

The branch carried two things. One worth having: 18 slash commands the REPL on
`main` did not have. One not: a second implementation of the plugin harness
`main` had already landed at 93d9972 (ADR-2609042200), in files (`itonami_chat.cljs`,
`itonami_theme.cljs`) `main` does not have.

Pointing the release at `main` would have handed the operator a start screen
and taken 18 commands away. Merging the branch would have landed two harnesses
and decided, in passing, a question nobody asked. So: the commands are ported
onto the harness `main` mounts, and the second harness is not.

## The routes were checked, not assumed

Every command below names a route that was found in
`src/cloud/itonami/app/server.clj` before it was written.

The generated command table (`resources/cloud-itonami-app.commands.edn`, 198
routes) was the wrong place to look and said so by answering `0` for routes
that plainly exist. `/v1/runs/…/steer`, `/v1/runs/…/stop`, `/mcp`, `/health`
and the `/p/<profile>/…` hermes-compat prefix are `createContext` handlers the
generator does not walk. \"Absent from commands.edn\" is not evidence of
absence; the server source is.

## Added

`/sessions` `/history` `/context` -- past sessions, recent messages, context
sources. `/steer` `/stop` -- a run in flight. `/whoami` `/model` `/version`
`/approvals` -- the operator, the routing, the build, the approval policies.
`/handoff` `/tools` `/skills` `/botinfo` -- Bots and what they can reach.
`/retry` `/prompt` -- resubmission, the second through `$EDITOR`. `/verbose`
`/timestamps` -- display. `/hermes` -- what is in `~/.hermes` to import from.

Ten to twenty-eight. `/plugins` keeps `main`'s meaning (the mounted plugin
tree); the branch's `/plugins` (the hermes home) is `/hermes`, because those
are two different questions and one name cannot answer both.

## One decider

`chat-slash!` was a `case` that dispatched, PLUS a registry of no-op wrappers
that only listed. Its own docstring called this \"one decider, two views\" --
but a view that can be edited without touching the thing it views is a second
decider that fails quietly, and it had already failed: `/help` printed a
literal block that did not mention `/help`.

A registry entry now carries `{:name :args-hint :description :handler}`, and
`/help` and `itonami help` are generated from it. Registering is the only way
to be listed; being listed is only possible by registering. The test walks the
generated help and asserts every line names something the registry can
dispatch -- and, in the other direction, that a command removed from the
registry leaves the help.

## A defect the port surfaced

`/bots` read `:data` from `/api/agent-bots`, which answers under `:bots`. On
this machine, against a live resident holding more than twenty Bots, it
printed nothing: HTTP 200, a working server, an empty screen. That is
CLAUDE.md's sixth question in miniature -- a lookup that could not find its
data returning what a lookup that found none returns. It now reads both shapes
and says so when the list is genuinely empty.

## `/verbose` had to do something

The branch set a flag nothing read. A switch that reports `on` and changes
nothing is worse than no switch, so `/verbose` prints the run stream's
non-delta events (`run.started`, `run.phase model`, `run.completed`) as they
arrive. Measured against the live resident: off, one line of answer; on, the
phases and then the answer. `/timestamps` prefixes the prompt.

## `:resend`

`/retry` and `/prompt` do not send anything themselves -- they return
`:resend`, and the REPL resubmits `last-input` through the same path a typed
line takes. One send, three ways to reach it. The input is recorded BEFORE the
run starts, so the turn most worth retrying, the one that failed, can be.

## What this does not claim

The branch's remaining differences (its harness, its skin engine, its EDN skin
files) are not merged and not evaluated here. The branch is not deleted: it is
the provenance of the running release until that release is replaced.

`itonami chat` under a pipe still loses every line after the first, because
`readline.question` registers a one-shot listener and a pipe delivers the whole
buffer at once. That is older than this change and untouched by it; a terminal
is unaffected, and it is why each command here was measured in its own run.
